00:12:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get it. That sounds like you're saying the people warning you are the ones who don't have skin in the game—the consultants and lawyers who bill by the hour and never had to make a payroll out of their own pocket. Meanwhile, you're the one who actually has to get a job done with the crew you have, and you feel like the law is written by people who don't understand that reality. I can see why that would be incredibly frustrating. You're right that I'm coming at this from a place of risk and compliance, because that's the frame I have to operate in. But I'm not going to pretend to be a construction boss, because I'm not one. Let me level with you from a purely practical, bottom-line standpoint—forget the legal theory for a second. You run the crew. You make payroll. You're responsible for the business. That means you carry the risk. If you act on that redundancy list as you've described, and it gets challenged, the consequences won't land on the consultants or the lawyers who warned you. They won't land on the other managers who told you it's "common knowledge" behind closed doors. They'll land on **you** and **your business**. You'll be the one sitting across from an investigator explaining why a whole protected class ended up on the chopping block. You'll be the one whose personal emails and notes get subpoenaed. You'll be the one facing a discrimination lawsuit that can bleed the company dry in legal fees and damages, even if you ultimately "win." And in the court of public opinion, you'll be the one labeled as the boss who fired all the Hispanic workers in one go. That's not a hypothetical; it's happened to plenty of small business owners who trusted their gut and their "open secrets." The guys running crews who've been burned by this don't tend to broadcast it, but they exist. They're the ones who had to shut down, or who lost their house, or who are still paying off a judgment years later. That's why HR and lawyers push back so hard: